# devices/pclm10/drivers/power/conf: this phone's own power drivers: the SMB5 charger and the gen4 gauge
# on its PM8150B (charging on this phone is managed on the application processor's side, not by the DSP),
# and the keys of its PM8150: the power key on the power-on block, the volume keys on its GPIO controller.
# CONFIG_HAKIOS_<NAME>=y in a defconfig builds a driver into the kernel; "standard" files are always built.
# tools/hakios-config.py reads this file; docs/drivers-config.md says what each word means and how it reaches XNU.

driver SMB5
	requires BATTERY
	file    bsd/dev/hakios.smb5.c
	help    the SMB5 charger and the gen4 fuel gauge on this phone's PM8150B, read through the PMIC
	help    arbiter (smb5=1): which stage the charge is in, what the USB port found, and the battery's
	help    voltage and current, handed to the battery monitor and the power source Darwin reads

driver PON
	file    bsd/dev/hakios.pon.c
	help    the keys of this phone, read through the PMIC arbiter (pon=1): the power key on the
	help    power-on block of its PM8150, the older single kind, subtype 0x04, whose KPDPWR is bit 0
	help    of its status -- not the split PMK8350's that drivers/input/hakios.buttons.c knows --
	help    and both volume keys on that chip's GPIO controller, blocks 0xc500 and 0xc600, which
	help    the driver enables as inputs with a pull-up and reads; the block's RESIN input is watched
	help    too and says what it sees, but it is not this phone's volume down (logs/093440-run.log)

driver BQ27541
	file    bsd/dev/hakios.bq27541.c
	help    the pack's own gauge (bq27541=1): the BQ27541 the overlay puts at 0x55 on the
	help    i2c@0xc94000 bus, read by bit-banging that bus's two pins (gpio27/gpio28, east TLMM
	help    tile) -- the QUPv3 serial engine there reports its FIFO interface disabled and its
	help    vendor driver uses GPI DMA, so the pads are driven directly. The charge, voltage,
	help    current and temperature go to the SMB5 driver's battery report
